1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:02,300
Welcome to Module 2.

2
00:00:02,520 --> 00:00:05,870
In Module 1 you built a
solid Python foundation.

3
00:00:06,090 --> 00:00:11,552
Now we meet the engine behind every
agent in this course: the large language model.

4
00:00:11,772 --> 00:00:17,092
Before writing a single API call, you
need a clear mental model of what these

5
00:00:17,092 --> 00:00:20,961
systems do, and just as
importantly, what they don't do.

6
00:00:22,261 --> 00:00:25,994
You will be able to explain
next-token prediction in plain words.

7
00:00:26,344 --> 00:00:30,961
Understand tokens, and why they decide
your cost, your limits, and your speed.

8
00:00:31,311 --> 00:00:34,611
Reason about the context
window, and what fills it up.

9
00:00:34,961 --> 00:00:39,865
And know which controls you actually
have over generation, on current models.

10
00:00:41,165 --> 00:00:44,351
Here is the core idea,
simplified but accurate.

11
00:00:44,701 --> 00:00:47,752
A language model takes
the text so far as input.

12
00:00:47,972 --> 00:00:53,567
During training it saw an enormous
amount of text, and its weights encode patterns

13
00:00:53,567 --> 00:00:55,069
of how text continues.

14
00:00:55,419 --> 00:00:57,502
Its output is not a whole answer.

15
00:00:57,722 --> 00:01:00,800
It is a probability for
every possible next token.

16
00:01:01,020 --> 00:01:04,012
These percentages
are just an illustration.

17
00:01:04,362 --> 00:01:09,386
One token is chosen and appended to the
text, and the whole thing goes back in to

18
00:01:09,386 --> 00:01:10,688
predict the next one.

19
00:01:10,908 --> 00:01:15,393
A long answer is just this loop,
run hundreds or thousands of times.

20
00:01:15,743 --> 00:01:17,830
This explains a lot of behaviour.

21
00:01:18,050 --> 00:01:21,713
The model produces
plausible text, not looked-up facts.

22
00:01:21,933 --> 00:01:27,298
It can be confidently wrong, which is
often called hallucination, and its knowledge

23
00:01:27,298 --> 00:01:29,237
stops at its training cut-off.

24
00:01:29,457 --> 00:01:34,649
That's why agents give models tools and
documents to check facts, which is exactly

25
00:01:34,649 --> 00:01:36,169
what this course builds.

26
00:01:37,469 --> 00:01:38,812
So what is a token?

27
00:01:39,032 --> 00:01:44,626
It's a chunk of text, often a whole
short word, a piece of a longer word, a space,

28
00:01:44,626 --> 00:01:46,127
or a punctuation mark.

29
00:01:46,477 --> 00:01:51,740
For example, agents call tools, full
stop, might be split into four tokens.

30
00:01:51,960 --> 00:01:53,532
This is an illustration.

31
00:01:53,752 --> 00:01:57,211
The exact split depends
on each model's tokenizer.

32
00:01:57,561 --> 00:02:01,930
A rough rule of thumb for English
is about four characters per token.

33
00:02:02,150 --> 00:02:07,734
Code, unusual words, and many other
languages use more tokens for the same text.

34
00:02:08,084 --> 00:02:12,793
When you need an exact number, don't
guess, and don't use another company's

35
00:02:12,793 --> 00:02:13,421
tokenizer.

36
00:02:13,641 --> 00:02:19,233
Ask the API: it has a token counting
endpoint for the exact model you're using.

37
00:02:19,453 --> 00:02:21,233
You'll use it in lesson five.

38
00:02:22,533 --> 00:02:24,591
Tokens matter for three reasons.

39
00:02:24,811 --> 00:02:25,772
First, cost.

40
00:02:25,992 --> 00:02:31,862
You pay per input token and per output
token, and output tokens usually cost several

41
00:02:31,862 --> 00:02:32,631
times more.

42
00:02:32,981 --> 00:02:34,037
Second, limits.

43
00:02:34,257 --> 00:02:38,105
Every request has a
maximum size, measured in tokens.

44
00:02:38,455 --> 00:02:39,606
And third, speed.

45
00:02:39,826 --> 00:02:45,118
Replies are generated one token at
a time, so long answers take longer.

46
00:02:45,338 --> 00:02:48,912
Streaming, in lesson four,
makes that wait feel much shorter.

47
00:02:50,212 --> 00:02:55,739
The context window is the maximum
number of tokens the model can consider in one

48
00:02:55,739 --> 00:02:58,158
request, input and output together.

49
00:02:58,508 --> 00:03:04,038
Everything you send goes in: the system
prompt with your instructions, the whole

50
00:03:04,038 --> 00:03:09,016
conversation so far, any documents
or tool results, and the new message.

51
00:03:09,366 --> 00:03:12,153
Current models have very large windows.

52
00:03:12,373 --> 00:03:19,092
Claude Opus 5.5, the model we use in
this course, accepts up to one million tokens.

53
00:03:19,442 --> 00:03:24,865
The reply must fit too, and it has its
own cap: up to one hundred and twenty-eight

54
00:03:24,865 --> 00:03:27,313
thousand output tokens on this model.

55
00:03:27,663 --> 00:03:30,337
A huge window does not
mean you should fill it.

56
00:03:30,557 --> 00:03:36,281
Every token you send is billed on every
request, and focused context usually gives

57
00:03:36,281 --> 00:03:37,328
better answers.

58
00:03:37,548 --> 00:03:42,194
Managing what goes into the
window is a core agent engineering skill.

59
00:03:43,494 --> 00:03:44,986
One more crucial fact.

60
00:03:45,206 --> 00:03:49,395
When you chat with an assistant, it
seems to remember what you said earlier.

61
00:03:49,745 --> 00:03:53,160
But the model itself has
no memory between requests.

62
00:03:53,380 --> 00:03:57,110
Your application sends the
whole conversation every time.

63
00:03:57,330 --> 00:03:59,674
The model's weights
don't change while you chat.

64
00:03:59,894 --> 00:04:03,973
If your code forgets to send the
history, the conversation is gone.

65
00:04:04,193 --> 00:04:06,193
You'll see this happen in lesson three.

66
00:04:07,493 --> 00:04:10,086
You control generation with a few levers.

67
00:04:10,306 --> 00:04:13,447
The system prompt sets
the role, rules, and style.

68
00:04:13,797 --> 00:04:16,223
Max tokens caps the length of the reply.

69
00:04:16,573 --> 00:04:20,963
Effort controls how much
reasoning the model does, from low to max.

70
00:04:21,183 --> 00:04:25,905
Higher effort means better
answers on hard problems, but more tokens.

71
00:04:26,255 --> 00:04:32,185
And you choose the model itself: Opus,
Sonnet, or Haiku, trading capability against

72
00:04:32,185 --> 00:04:33,257
cost and speed.

73
00:04:33,607 --> 00:04:37,770
You may have read about
temperature and top p in older tutorials.

74
00:04:37,990 --> 00:04:42,496
On current Claude models those
sampling settings are no longer accepted.

75
00:04:42,716 --> 00:04:46,688
You steer the model with
instructions, effort, and the model you pick.

76
00:04:47,988 --> 00:04:48,795
Quick check.

77
00:04:49,015 --> 00:04:52,493
Your app sends a new request
without the earlier messages.

78
00:04:52,713 --> 00:04:53,657
What happens?

79
00:04:59,357 --> 00:05:01,049
The model simply doesn't know.

80
00:05:01,269 --> 00:05:03,418
It has no memory between requests.

81
00:05:03,638 --> 00:05:06,193
It only sees what
you send in that request.

82
00:05:07,493 --> 00:05:11,070
A language model predicts
the next token, over and over.

83
00:05:11,420 --> 00:05:16,913
It produces plausible text, not
looked-up facts, so important claims need checking.

84
00:05:17,263 --> 00:05:22,564
Tokens drive cost, limits, and
speed, and the API can count them exactly.

85
00:05:22,914 --> 00:05:28,090
Everything must fit in the context
window, and your app resends the history every

86
00:05:28,090 --> 00:05:28,410
time.

87
00:05:28,760 --> 00:05:34,569
Next lesson, you make your first real API
call, and take the response apart piece by

88
00:05:34,569 --> 00:05:34,984
piece.

89
00:05:36,295 --> 00:05:38,483
Time to talk to a model from Python.

90
00:05:38,704 --> 00:05:44,510
In this lesson you'll set up an API key
safely, install the official Anthropic SDK,

91
00:05:44,510 --> 00:05:50,387
send your first message, and then take
the response apart, because every field in it

92
00:05:50,387 --> 00:05:52,486
matters when you build agents.

93
00:05:53,786 --> 00:05:56,525
You'll create and store an API key safely.

94
00:05:56,875 --> 00:05:59,905
Install the official Anthropic Python SDK.

95
00:06:00,255 --> 00:06:02,786
Send a request with messages dot create.

96
00:06:03,136 --> 00:06:08,413
And read the response: its content
blocks, its stop reason, and its token usage.

97
00:06:09,713 --> 00:06:12,778
First, create an API
key in the Claude Console.

98
00:06:12,998 --> 00:06:17,606
Note that API usage is billed
separately from a Claude chat subscription.

99
00:06:17,956 --> 00:06:22,807
Store it in an environment
variable called ANTHROPIC API KEY.

100
00:06:23,027 --> 00:06:28,518
The SDK reads that variable
automatically, so the key never appears in your code.

101
00:06:28,868 --> 00:06:33,330
Never paste a key into code, a
chat, a screenshot, or a git commit.

102
00:06:33,550 --> 00:06:38,485
If a key ever leaks, delete it in the
Console straight away and create a new one.

103
00:06:38,835 --> 00:06:39,823
No key yet?

104
00:06:40,043 --> 00:06:41,036
No problem.

105
00:06:41,256 --> 00:06:46,953
Everything in this module also runs in
offline mode, which I'll explain in a moment.

106
00:06:48,253 --> 00:06:51,435
As always, start with a
fresh virtual environment.

107
00:06:51,785 --> 00:06:54,092
Then install the anthropic package.

108
00:06:54,312 --> 00:06:59,893
I'm pinning version 2 point 3 point 0,
the version this course was recorded with.

109
00:07:00,113 --> 00:07:06,266
It brings its own HTTP library and
pydantic, which we'll use for structured output.

110
00:07:06,616 --> 00:07:07,734
And set your key.

111
00:07:07,954 --> 00:07:13,775
On Windows PowerShell, use dollar
env colon ANTHROPIC API KEY instead.

112
00:07:13,995 --> 00:07:17,925
Or keep it in a dot env
file, as you learned in Module 1.

113
00:07:19,225 --> 00:07:21,083
An honest note about the demos.

114
00:07:21,303 --> 00:07:26,585
So that every demo is free and
repeatable, and so you can follow along without a

115
00:07:26,585 --> 00:07:31,802
key, the replies you'll see come from
a small fake API built into this module's

116
00:07:31,802 --> 00:07:32,330
project.

117
00:07:32,550 --> 00:07:39,147
It speaks the same protocol as the real
API, so the real SDK code runs unchanged,

118
00:07:39,147 --> 00:07:42,486
including streaming, errors, and retries.

119
00:07:42,706 --> 00:07:47,301
But the replies are placeholders,
clearly marked offline demo reply.

120
00:07:47,521 --> 00:07:49,072
They are not model output.

121
00:07:49,292 --> 00:07:52,994
With your key set, exactly
the same code talks to Claude.

122
00:07:54,294 --> 00:07:59,737
In your own projects, you create a
client with anthropic dot Anthropic, which reads

123
00:07:59,737 --> 00:08:01,639
the key from the environment.

124
00:08:01,859 --> 00:08:06,872
Our lesson helper does the same when a
key is set, and uses the offline fake when it

125
00:08:06,872 --> 00:08:07,230
isn't.

126
00:08:07,580 --> 00:08:10,029
Messages dot create sends a request.

127
00:08:10,249 --> 00:08:16,204
Three arguments are required: the model
ID, max tokens, the longest reply you'll

128
00:08:16,204 --> 00:08:18,661
accept, and the list of messages.

129
00:08:18,881 --> 00:08:24,457
Each message is a dictionary with a role
and content, exactly the shape you built in

130
00:08:24,457 --> 00:08:25,055
Module 1.

131
00:08:25,405 --> 00:08:27,865
The reply's content is a list of blocks.

132
00:08:28,085 --> 00:08:31,337
Here there's a single text
block, so we print its text.

133
00:08:31,687 --> 00:08:36,778
And we print two more fields: why the
model stopped, and how many tokens went in and

134
00:08:36,778 --> 00:08:37,021
out.

135
00:08:38,321 --> 00:08:40,404
Here's the run, in offline mode.

136
00:08:40,624 --> 00:08:46,054
A placeholder reply, a stop reason of
end turn, which means the model finished

137
00:08:46,054 --> 00:08:48,281
naturally, and the token counts.

138
00:08:48,501 --> 00:08:52,135
With a real key you'd see a
real one-sentence answer here.

139
00:08:53,435 --> 00:08:57,001
Let's explore the response
object in the interactive prompt.

140
00:08:57,351 --> 00:09:02,752
Every response has an ID, which you
include when reporting problems, the model that

141
00:09:02,752 --> 00:09:04,964
answered, and the role, assistant.

142
00:09:05,184 --> 00:09:07,380
Content is a list of typed blocks.

143
00:09:07,730 --> 00:09:09,786
The stop reason and the usage.

144
00:09:10,006 --> 00:09:14,326
Usage is what you are billed for,
so production code always records it.

145
00:09:14,546 --> 00:09:16,525
We'll do exactly that in the project.

146
00:09:17,825 --> 00:09:19,248
Why is content a list?

147
00:09:19,468 --> 00:09:22,463
Because a reply can
contain different kinds of block.

148
00:09:22,683 --> 00:09:24,682
Text blocks hold the written answer.

149
00:09:25,032 --> 00:09:27,485
Thinking blocks can
hold the model's reasoning.

150
00:09:27,705 --> 00:09:32,252
On current models it's hidden by
default, but the block may still be there.

151
00:09:32,602 --> 00:09:38,008
And tool use blocks are requests to run
one of your tools, which is how agents act.

152
00:09:38,228 --> 00:09:40,406
You'll handle those in Module 5.

153
00:09:40,756 --> 00:09:44,398
So robust code never
assumes the first block is text.

154
00:09:44,618 --> 00:09:48,121
It checks each block's
type, as the next example does.

155
00:09:49,421 --> 00:09:54,673
The system parameter gives the model its
role and rules for the whole conversation.

156
00:09:54,893 --> 00:09:56,997
It sits outside the messages list.

157
00:09:57,217 --> 00:10:01,216
Here: be a patient tutor, and
answer in at most two sentences.

158
00:10:01,566 --> 00:10:06,556
And here's the robust way to read the
answer: loop over the blocks and only print

159
00:10:06,556 --> 00:10:07,295
text blocks.

160
00:10:08,595 --> 00:10:13,196
In offline mode the placeholder
can't follow the instructions, of course.

161
00:10:13,416 --> 00:10:16,039
A real model would
answer in two sentences.

162
00:10:16,259 --> 00:10:20,238
Prompt design gets a whole
module of its own, Module 3.

163
00:10:21,538 --> 00:10:22,346
Quick check.

164
00:10:22,566 --> 00:10:26,404
Which three arguments does
messages dot create require?

165
00:10:32,104 --> 00:10:34,437
Model, max tokens, and messages.

166
00:10:34,657 --> 00:10:38,731
The key comes from the
environment, and the system prompt is optional.

167
00:10:40,031 --> 00:10:44,328
API keys live in an
environment variable, never in your code.

168
00:10:44,678 --> 00:10:49,225
Messages dot create needs a
model, max tokens, and messages.

169
00:10:49,575 --> 00:10:54,252
The response gives you content
blocks, a stop reason, and token usage.

170
00:10:54,602 --> 00:10:57,267
And the system prompt
sets the role and the rules.

171
00:10:57,617 --> 00:10:59,596
Next lesson: conversations.

172
00:10:59,816 --> 00:11:04,631
You'll see exactly why the model
forgets, and how to manage message history

173
00:11:04,631 --> 00:11:05,209
properly.

174
00:11:06,514 --> 00:11:12,479
Every chat app and every agent is a
conversation: a list of messages that grows over

175
00:11:12,479 --> 00:11:12,834
time.

176
00:11:13,054 --> 00:11:18,123
In this lesson you'll see that the API
remembers nothing, that your code owns the

177
00:11:18,123 --> 00:11:21,690
history, and that a growing
history means a growing bill.

178
00:11:22,990 --> 00:11:25,929
You'll build a
multi-turn conversation correctly.

179
00:11:26,279 --> 00:11:29,080
Spot and fix the
classic forgotten reply bug.

180
00:11:29,430 --> 00:11:32,281
Measure how input
tokens grow with every turn.

181
00:11:32,631 --> 00:11:36,712
And choose a strategy for
keeping long conversations under control.

182
00:11:38,012 --> 00:11:40,291
Here's how a conversation really works.

183
00:11:40,641 --> 00:11:43,461
The first request
contains one user message.

184
00:11:43,811 --> 00:11:46,873
The API replies, and
then forgets everything.

185
00:11:47,093 --> 00:11:50,726
Your app stores the user
message and the reply in a list.

186
00:11:51,076 --> 00:11:56,087
The second request sends the whole
list: the first question, the first reply, and

187
00:11:56,087 --> 00:11:57,138
the new question.

188
00:11:57,488 --> 00:11:59,992
And the third request sends five messages.

189
00:12:00,212 --> 00:12:05,644
Roles alternate between user and
assistant, and the first message is always from the

190
00:12:05,644 --> 00:12:05,967
user.

191
00:12:07,267 --> 00:12:11,096
The history is just a Python
list of message dictionaries.

192
00:12:11,446 --> 00:12:16,446
For each question, we append it to the
history, and send the whole list as messages.

193
00:12:16,796 --> 00:12:18,928
Then we append the assistant's reply.

194
00:12:19,148 --> 00:12:21,295
This line is the one people forget.

195
00:12:21,645 --> 00:12:27,008
Finally we print how many messages we
sent, and how many input tokens that cost.

196
00:12:28,308 --> 00:12:29,451
Look at the numbers.

197
00:12:29,671 --> 00:12:31,876
One message, then three, then five.

198
00:12:32,096 --> 00:12:37,212
And the input tokens grow every turn,
because every request re-sends everything

199
00:12:37,212 --> 00:12:37,860
before it.

200
00:12:38,080 --> 00:12:43,733
The offline fake even reports how many
earlier messages it received, so you can see

201
00:12:43,733 --> 00:12:45,163
the history arriving.

202
00:12:46,463 --> 00:12:47,789
Here's the classic bug.

203
00:12:48,009 --> 00:12:51,258
The loop appends each
question, but never the reply.

204
00:12:51,608 --> 00:12:54,756
So the history contains
two user messages in a row.

205
00:12:54,976 --> 00:12:59,336
The API accepts that, and
merges them into a single user turn.

206
00:12:59,556 --> 00:13:04,777
But the model never sees what it said
before, so it can't refer to its own earlier

207
00:13:04,777 --> 00:13:05,287
answers.

208
00:13:06,587 --> 00:13:07,652
User, user.

209
00:13:07,872 --> 00:13:09,147
No assistant turn.

210
00:13:09,367 --> 00:13:13,724
In a chat app, that shows up as an
assistant that seems to have amnesia.

211
00:13:13,944 --> 00:13:16,952
Always store the reply
right after you receive it.

212
00:13:18,252 --> 00:13:23,772
Since every turn re-sends the whole
history, long conversations get slower and more

213
00:13:23,772 --> 00:13:27,032
expensive, and
eventually hit the context window.

214
00:13:27,252 --> 00:13:29,189
There are a few standard strategies.

215
00:13:29,539 --> 00:13:34,907
The simplest is a sliding window: keep
the most recent turns, and drop the oldest

216
00:13:34,907 --> 00:13:38,220
user and assistant pair
once you're over a budget.

217
00:13:38,440 --> 00:13:39,886
Our project does this.

218
00:13:40,236 --> 00:13:45,341
A smarter option replaces old turns
with a short summary written by the model.

219
00:13:45,561 --> 00:13:47,698
That's part of Module 6 on memory.

220
00:13:48,048 --> 00:13:53,432
And agents often store key facts in a
database, and add back only what's relevant to

221
00:13:53,432 --> 00:13:54,778
the current question.

222
00:13:55,128 --> 00:14:00,546
Finally, prompt caching lets an
unchanged prefix of the conversation be billed at a

223
00:14:00,546 --> 00:14:02,830
large discount when you re-send it.

224
00:14:03,050 --> 00:14:04,881
You'll use it later in the course.

225
00:14:06,181 --> 00:14:08,063
One practical rule when trimming.

226
00:14:08,283 --> 00:14:13,699
Drop messages in pairs, a user message
and its reply together, so the roles keep

227
00:14:13,699 --> 00:14:17,626
alternating and the history
still starts with a user turn.

228
00:14:18,926 --> 00:14:19,733
Quick check.

229
00:14:19,953 --> 00:14:22,740
A conversation already has ten messages.

230
00:14:22,960 --> 00:14:25,746
How many messages does
your next request send?

231
00:14:31,446 --> 00:14:36,927
Eleven: all ten earlier messages plus
the new one, unless you deliberately trim the

232
00:14:36,927 --> 00:14:37,456
history.

233
00:14:38,756 --> 00:14:42,227
The API is stateless, and
your code keeps the history.

234
00:14:42,577 --> 00:14:45,706
Append both the question
and the reply, every turn.

235
00:14:46,056 --> 00:14:49,168
Input tokens, and
cost, grow with every turn.

236
00:14:49,518 --> 00:14:54,223
So trim in pairs, or summarise,
to keep conversations under control.

237
00:14:54,573 --> 00:14:59,683
Next, you'll control the output itself:
length limits, streaming, and structured

238
00:14:59,683 --> 00:15:00,003
JSON.

239
00:15:01,330 --> 00:15:02,924
Getting a reply is easy.

240
00:15:03,144 --> 00:15:06,775
Getting a reply your
program can rely on takes more care.

241
00:15:06,995 --> 00:15:12,418
In this lesson you'll control how long
replies are, show them as they're generated,

242
00:15:12,418 --> 00:15:17,906
get structured JSON instead of free text,
and handle the cases where the model stops

243
00:15:17,906 --> 00:15:18,298
early.

244
00:15:19,598 --> 00:15:23,460
You'll use max tokens, and
check the stop reason every time.

245
00:15:23,810 --> 00:15:26,443
Stream replies for a responsive interface.

246
00:15:26,793 --> 00:15:29,676
Get validated JSON
with structured outputs.

247
00:15:30,026 --> 00:15:31,771
And handle refusals safely.

248
00:15:33,071 --> 00:15:35,383
Every response tells you why it ended.

249
00:15:35,603 --> 00:15:38,937
End turn means the model
finished its answer naturally.

250
00:15:39,287 --> 00:15:44,010
Max tokens means it hit your limit
and was cut off, possibly mid-sentence.

251
00:15:44,360 --> 00:15:48,385
Refusal means the model
declined the request for safety reasons.

252
00:15:48,735 --> 00:15:53,426
Stop sequence means it produced one
of the custom stop strings you supplied.

253
00:15:53,776 --> 00:15:56,785
Tool use means it wants
to run one of your tools.

254
00:15:57,005 --> 00:15:59,815
That's the heart of agents, in Module 5.

255
00:16:00,165 --> 00:16:05,041
And pause turn means a long agentic
turn paused, and you can continue it.

256
00:16:06,341 --> 00:16:11,700
We ask the same question twice, once
with a generous limit and once with only

257
00:16:11,700 --> 00:16:12,744
fifteen tokens.

258
00:16:13,094 --> 00:16:14,711
And we check the stop reason.

259
00:16:14,931 --> 00:16:20,545
A cut-off reply looks like a normal
string, so if you don't check, you'll silently

260
00:16:20,545 --> 00:16:24,927
show users half an answer, or
try to parse half a JSON document.

261
00:16:26,227 --> 00:16:28,511
With a thousand tokens, end turn.

262
00:16:28,731 --> 00:16:34,670
With fifteen, the stop reason is max
tokens, and exactly fifteen output tokens were

263
00:16:34,670 --> 00:16:35,028
used.

264
00:16:35,248 --> 00:16:37,251
The model doesn't plan to fit the limit.

265
00:16:37,471 --> 00:16:38,640
It's simply stopped.

266
00:16:38,990 --> 00:16:44,443
So set max tokens high enough for the
job, and treat max tokens as the stop reason

267
00:16:44,443 --> 00:16:45,773
that needs handling.

268
00:16:45,993 --> 00:16:47,827
Don't lowball it to save money.

269
00:16:48,047 --> 00:16:51,190
A truncated answer
usually costs you a retry.

270
00:16:52,490 --> 00:16:55,772
Remember that models
generate one token at a time.

271
00:16:55,992 --> 00:17:01,329
Without streaming, your program waits
for the entire reply, with nothing on screen.

272
00:17:01,679 --> 00:17:07,398
With streaming, the API sends pieces as
they're generated, so text appears almost

273
00:17:07,398 --> 00:17:08,246
immediately.

274
00:17:08,466 --> 00:17:11,972
The total time is the
same, but it feels far faster.

275
00:17:12,192 --> 00:17:16,566
Streaming also avoids request
timeouts on very long replies.

276
00:17:17,866 --> 00:17:23,402
Messages dot stream takes exactly the
same arguments as create, and is used as a

277
00:17:23,402 --> 00:17:25,202
context manager with with.

278
00:17:25,552 --> 00:17:28,866
Text stream yields small
pieces of text as they arrive.

279
00:17:29,086 --> 00:17:33,975
We print each one immediately, with
flush equals True so it isn't held in a buffer.

280
00:17:34,325 --> 00:17:39,691
And when the stream ends, get final
message gives you the complete message object,

281
00:17:39,691 --> 00:17:42,898
with its stop reason
and usage, just like create.

282
00:17:44,198 --> 00:17:50,024
In the video the pieces arrive
instantly, because the fake API is local, but the

283
00:17:50,024 --> 00:17:55,559
code path is the real streaming code:
server-sent events, parsed by the SDK.

284
00:17:56,859 --> 00:18:02,495
Agents constantly need machine-readable
output: a quiz, a plan, a tool decision.

285
00:18:02,715 --> 00:18:05,939
Just asking for JSON
in the prompt is fragile.

286
00:18:06,159 --> 00:18:10,204
Extra words, wrong keys, or
broken JSON can slip through.

287
00:18:10,554 --> 00:18:12,321
Structured outputs fix this.

288
00:18:12,541 --> 00:18:18,621
You pass a JSON schema, and the API
constrains the reply to valid JSON for that

289
00:18:18,621 --> 00:18:19,160
schema.

290
00:18:19,380 --> 00:18:25,605
With a Pydantic class, the SDK builds
the schema and parses the reply into a Python

291
00:18:25,605 --> 00:18:26,730
object for you.

292
00:18:28,030 --> 00:18:33,664
First, describe the shape with Pydantic
classes: a question has text, four options,

293
00:18:33,664 --> 00:18:37,058
the index of the right
answer, and an explanation.

294
00:18:37,278 --> 00:18:40,093
A quiz has a topic
and a list of questions.

295
00:18:40,313 --> 00:18:43,296
Pydantic gets a full lesson in Module 3.

296
00:18:43,516 --> 00:18:46,283
For now, read these as typed dataclasses.

297
00:18:46,633 --> 00:18:52,060
These Field constraints say exactly
four options and an index from zero to three.

298
00:18:52,280 --> 00:18:57,756
The API's schema support is limited, so
the SDK moves those limits into the field

299
00:18:57,756 --> 00:19:02,150
descriptions, and then checks
them itself when the reply arrives.

300
00:19:02,500 --> 00:19:07,922
Then call messages dot parse instead of
create, with output format set to the class.

301
00:19:08,272 --> 00:19:13,763
Parsed output is a real, validated Quiz
object, so you use attributes instead of

302
00:19:13,763 --> 00:19:15,753
digging through dictionaries.

303
00:19:17,053 --> 00:19:23,170
The offline fake returns schema-valid
placeholder questions, and they come back as a

304
00:19:23,170 --> 00:19:24,408
real Quiz object.

305
00:19:24,628 --> 00:19:30,458
If a reply doesn't fit the schema, the
SDK raises a validation error instead of

306
00:19:30,458 --> 00:19:32,008
handing you bad data.

307
00:19:33,308 --> 00:19:34,885
Last case: refusals.

308
00:19:35,105 --> 00:19:40,711
If the stop reason is refusal, the
model declined the request for safety reasons,

309
00:19:40,711 --> 00:19:42,718
and the content may be empty.

310
00:19:42,938 --> 00:19:45,824
So check the stop reason
before you read the text.

311
00:19:46,044 --> 00:19:51,671
On current models, the API can also
automatically re-run a declined request on

312
00:19:51,671 --> 00:19:54,700
another model, a feature called fallbacks.

313
00:19:54,920 --> 00:19:58,729
Our project turns it on, and
you'll see the code in lesson six.

314
00:20:00,029 --> 00:20:00,837
Quick check.

315
00:20:01,057 --> 00:20:05,924
Your code parses the reply as
JSON, and it fails on a long reply.

316
00:20:06,144 --> 00:20:07,503
What should you check first?

317
00:20:13,203 --> 00:20:15,669
Whether the stop reason is max tokens.

318
00:20:15,889 --> 00:20:18,927
A cut-off reply ends
in the middle of the JSON.

319
00:20:20,227 --> 00:20:23,595
The stop reason tells you
whether the reply is complete.

320
00:20:23,815 --> 00:20:24,791
Always check it.

321
00:20:25,141 --> 00:20:26,969
Stream for responsive output.

322
00:20:27,319 --> 00:20:31,944
Use structured outputs, with
messages dot parse, for validated JSON.

323
00:20:32,294 --> 00:20:35,217
And handle refusals
before reading the content.

324
00:20:35,567 --> 00:20:37,709
Next lesson: production basics.

325
00:20:37,929 --> 00:20:42,102
API keys, errors, rate limits,
retries, and what it all costs.

326
00:20:43,410 --> 00:20:45,379
A demo that works once is easy.

327
00:20:45,599 --> 00:20:50,925
A program that keeps working when the
network drops, when you hit a rate limit, or

328
00:20:50,925 --> 00:20:54,238
when costs start to
climb, takes deliberate design.

329
00:20:54,458 --> 00:20:59,233
This lesson covers the production
basics every AI application needs.

330
00:21:00,533 --> 00:21:05,160
You'll recognise the API's error
types, and know what to do about each one.

331
00:21:05,510 --> 00:21:07,244
Catch errors in the right order.

332
00:21:07,594 --> 00:21:11,775
Understand rate limits, and
the SDK's automatic retries.

333
00:21:12,125 --> 00:21:15,201
And calculate and
control what each call costs.

334
00:21:16,501 --> 00:21:18,014
Errors fall into groups.

335
00:21:18,234 --> 00:21:24,132
A four hundred or four oh four means
your request is wrong: a bad parameter, or a

336
00:21:24,132 --> 00:21:26,316
model name that doesn't exist.

337
00:21:26,536 --> 00:21:27,856
Retrying won't help.

338
00:21:28,076 --> 00:21:29,255
Fix the request.

339
00:21:29,605 --> 00:21:34,498
Four oh one and four oh three are
about your key: missing, wrong, or without

340
00:21:34,498 --> 00:21:35,206
permission.

341
00:21:35,426 --> 00:21:36,737
Again, don't retry.

342
00:21:36,957 --> 00:21:37,969
Fix the key.

343
00:21:38,319 --> 00:21:41,043
Four twenty-nine means
you've hit a rate limit.

344
00:21:41,263 --> 00:21:43,823
Waiting and retrying is exactly right.

345
00:21:44,173 --> 00:21:49,734
Five hundreds, including five
twenty-nine for overloaded, are server-side problems

346
00:21:49,734 --> 00:21:51,429
that are often temporary.

347
00:21:51,649 --> 00:21:53,480
Retry after a short wait.

348
00:21:53,830 --> 00:21:58,736
And connection errors and timeouts
mean no proper response came back at all.

349
00:21:58,956 --> 00:22:00,796
Those are also worth a retry.

350
00:22:01,146 --> 00:22:06,034
The rule is simple: retry rate
limits, server errors, and network problems.

351
00:22:06,254 --> 00:22:09,245
Never blindly retry
the other four hundreds.

352
00:22:10,545 --> 00:22:16,492
For this demo only, we turn off
automatic retries, so every error reaches our code.

353
00:22:16,842 --> 00:22:19,019
The call goes inside a try block.

354
00:22:19,369 --> 00:22:24,445
Then we catch specific exception
classes, most specific first: an authentication

355
00:22:24,445 --> 00:22:26,413
error, then a rate limit error.

356
00:22:26,763 --> 00:22:33,002
API status error is the parent class of
every HTTP error, so it catches anything we

357
00:22:33,002 --> 00:22:36,460
haven't handled, and
gives us the status code.

358
00:22:36,680 --> 00:22:41,819
Connection errors aren't HTTP errors
at all, so they have their own class.

359
00:22:42,039 --> 00:22:45,770
If you catch the parent first,
the specific handlers never run.

360
00:22:47,070 --> 00:22:52,563
To demonstrate errors without breaking
anything, the fake API reads an environment

361
00:22:52,563 --> 00:22:54,707
variable telling it how to fail.

362
00:22:55,057 --> 00:22:58,200
With no failure, a
normal placeholder reply.

363
00:22:58,550 --> 00:23:01,437
A four oh one becomes, check your API key.

364
00:23:01,787 --> 00:23:04,558
A four twenty-nine becomes, rate limited.

365
00:23:04,908 --> 00:23:10,213
And a five twenty-nine, an overloaded
server, falls through to the general status

366
00:23:10,213 --> 00:23:10,737
handler.

367
00:23:12,037 --> 00:23:17,240
Every API account has rate limits: how
many requests and tokens you may use per

368
00:23:17,240 --> 00:23:17,701
minute.

369
00:23:17,921 --> 00:23:20,640
When you exceed them,
you get a four twenty-nine.

370
00:23:20,990 --> 00:23:24,909
The standard response is to
retry with exponential backoff.

371
00:23:25,259 --> 00:23:30,667
Wait a short time, plus a little
randomness so that many clients don't all retry at

372
00:23:30,667 --> 00:23:32,686
the same moment, and try again.

373
00:23:33,036 --> 00:23:36,001
If it fails again,
wait roughly twice as long.

374
00:23:36,221 --> 00:23:40,578
Good news: the Anthropic SDK
already does all of this for you.

375
00:23:40,798 --> 00:23:46,597
It retries rate limits, server errors,
and connection errors twice by default, with

376
00:23:46,597 --> 00:23:50,928
backoff, and it respects the
retry after header the API sends.

377
00:23:52,228 --> 00:23:56,439
You control this with max
retries, on the client or per request.

378
00:23:56,659 --> 00:23:59,418
Two is the default, and zero turns it off.

379
00:24:00,718 --> 00:24:05,411
Here the fake API fails with a four
twenty-nine and then a five hundred.

380
00:24:05,631 --> 00:24:12,103
Setting ANTHROPIC LOG to info shows what
the SDK does: it waits about half a second,

381
00:24:12,103 --> 00:24:16,650
retries, waits about a
second, retries again, and succeeds.

382
00:24:16,870 --> 00:24:19,208
Our code didn't need a single extra line.

383
00:24:20,508 --> 00:24:22,479
Set a timeout you're comfortable with.

384
00:24:22,699 --> 00:24:24,973
The SDK default is ten minutes.

385
00:24:25,193 --> 00:24:27,757
For long replies,
stream instead of waiting.

386
00:24:28,107 --> 00:24:30,966
When something fails, log the request ID.

387
00:24:31,186 --> 00:24:34,270
It identifies exactly
which request went wrong.

388
00:24:34,620 --> 00:24:39,741
Use separate keys for separate apps and
environments, so you can revoke one without

389
00:24:39,741 --> 00:24:40,974
breaking the others.

390
00:24:41,324 --> 00:24:44,881
And never send keys to the
model, or print them in logs.

391
00:24:45,101 --> 00:24:47,911
Logs are often far less
protected than you think.

392
00:24:49,211 --> 00:24:50,062
Now, money.

393
00:24:50,282 --> 00:24:55,727
You pay per token, with separate prices
for input and output, quoted per million

394
00:24:55,727 --> 00:24:56,204
tokens.

395
00:24:56,554 --> 00:25:02,665
Claude Opus 5.5, the most capable of the
three and our default, costs four dollars

396
00:25:02,665 --> 00:25:07,361
per million input tokens, and
twenty per million output tokens.

397
00:25:07,711 --> 00:25:10,095
Sonnet 5.5 costs half that.

398
00:25:10,445 --> 00:25:15,271
And Haiku 5.5 is far
cheaper, for simple, high-volume work.

399
00:25:15,621 --> 00:25:19,168
These are list prices in October 2026.

400
00:25:19,388 --> 00:25:23,209
Prices change, so always
check the official pricing page.

401
00:25:23,429 --> 00:25:27,942
And notice that output tokens
cost five times more than input tokens.

402
00:25:29,242 --> 00:25:30,991
Here's the cost calculation.

403
00:25:31,211 --> 00:25:33,177
Prices are per million tokens.

404
00:25:33,527 --> 00:25:39,033
Count tokens measures a request before
you send it, so you can warn about, or block,

405
00:25:39,033 --> 00:25:42,245
a request that would be
too big or too expensive.

406
00:25:42,595 --> 00:25:45,880
After the call, usage
gives the actual tokens.

407
00:25:46,100 --> 00:25:49,729
Multiply each by its
price, and divide by a million.

408
00:25:50,079 --> 00:25:54,300
And then the important habit:
multiply by your expected traffic.

409
00:25:55,600 --> 00:25:57,419
A fraction of a cent per call.

410
00:25:57,639 --> 00:26:02,308
But the same call ten thousand
times a day costs real money every day.

411
00:26:02,528 --> 00:26:06,239
In offline mode these
token counts are rough estimates.

412
00:26:06,459 --> 00:26:08,290
With a real key, they're exact.

413
00:26:09,590 --> 00:26:11,475
Five ways to control cost.

414
00:26:11,695 --> 00:26:16,500
First, send less: trim history,
and include only relevant documents.

415
00:26:16,850 --> 00:26:20,937
Second, ask for concise
answers, with a sensible max tokens.

416
00:26:21,287 --> 00:26:23,846
Third, use lower effort for simple tasks.

417
00:26:24,196 --> 00:26:29,584
Fourth, pick the smallest model that
does the job well, measured on real examples,

418
00:26:29,584 --> 00:26:30,372
not guessed.

419
00:26:30,722 --> 00:26:35,429
And fifth, track spending in code,
with a budget that stops runaway loops.

420
00:26:35,649 --> 00:26:37,602
Our project does exactly this.

421
00:26:38,902 --> 00:26:39,710
Quick check.

422
00:26:39,930 --> 00:26:42,159
Which of these errors
should your code retry?

423
00:26:47,859 --> 00:26:49,258
The four twenty-nine.

424
00:26:49,478 --> 00:26:51,645
A rate limit recovers after waiting.

425
00:26:51,865 --> 00:26:56,667
The others fail the same way every
time, until you fix the request or the key.

426
00:26:57,967 --> 00:27:02,099
Four hundreds, except four
twenty-nine, need a fix, not a retry.

427
00:27:02,449 --> 00:27:08,145
Rate limits, server errors, and network
errors are retried with backoff, and the SDK

428
00:27:08,145 --> 00:27:09,365
does that for you.

429
00:27:09,715 --> 00:27:13,002
Catch specific exception
classes before the general one.

430
00:27:13,352 --> 00:27:17,473
And cost is tokens times
price, so count, track, and budget.

431
00:27:17,823 --> 00:27:20,358
You now know everything the project needs.

432
00:27:20,578 --> 00:27:24,140
Next lesson, we start
building the AI Study Assistant.

433
00:27:25,437 --> 00:27:26,512
Project time.

434
00:27:26,732 --> 00:27:32,176
Over two lessons you'll build the AI
Study Assistant: a command line tool that

435
00:27:32,176 --> 00:27:37,690
explains programming concepts, writes
quizzes as validated JSON, and chats with

436
00:27:37,690 --> 00:27:40,761
memory, while
tracking every cent it spends.

437
00:27:40,981 --> 00:27:43,358
Everything from this module appears in it.

438
00:27:44,658 --> 00:27:49,454
The explain command streams an
explanation of any topic, at a beginner,

439
00:27:49,454 --> 00:27:51,616
intermediate, or advanced level.

440
00:27:51,966 --> 00:27:57,671
The quiz command generates
multiple-choice questions as validated JSON, which you

441
00:27:57,671 --> 00:27:59,009
can save and grade.

442
00:27:59,359 --> 00:28:04,073
The chat command holds a conversation
with history, trimmed to a token budget.

443
00:28:04,423 --> 00:28:09,654
Every call reports its tokens and
cost, and a budget stops runaway spending.

444
00:28:10,004 --> 00:28:15,617
And it has clear errors, automatic
retries, and an offline mode, so the tests never

445
00:28:15,617 --> 00:28:17,375
need a key or the network.

446
00:28:18,675 --> 00:28:23,736
The command line handles arguments and
printing, and calls the assistant, which

447
00:28:23,736 --> 00:28:27,260
holds the logic for
explaining, quizzing, and chatting.

448
00:28:27,610 --> 00:28:32,433
Prompts live in their own module, so you
can improve them without touching the code.

449
00:28:32,653 --> 00:28:34,479
And models holds the quiz schema.

450
00:28:34,829 --> 00:28:38,136
Pricing calculates
costs and enforces the budget.

451
00:28:38,486 --> 00:28:44,844
And client builds the SDK client: a real
one when your key is set, or the same SDK

452
00:28:44,844 --> 00:28:48,411
pointed at the offline
fake API when it isn't.

453
00:28:48,631 --> 00:28:51,580
Nothing else in the
program knows the difference.

454
00:28:52,880 --> 00:28:57,309
Let's start with the piece that
makes testing possible: the fake API.

455
00:28:57,659 --> 00:29:03,327
It's a class whose objects can be
called like a function, once per HTTP request.

456
00:29:03,547 --> 00:29:09,081
A list of planned failures lets tests and
demos make it return a four twenty-nine or

457
00:29:09,081 --> 00:29:10,794
a five hundred on purpose.

458
00:29:11,144 --> 00:29:18,311
Each call receives the real HTTP
request the SDK built, and parses its JSON body.

459
00:29:18,531 --> 00:29:23,882
It records the body, so tests can check
exactly what was sent, and it answers token

460
00:29:23,882 --> 00:29:26,462
counting requests with a rough estimate.

461
00:29:26,812 --> 00:29:32,986
Then it prepares a placeholder reply,
applies any planned failure or refusal, cuts

462
00:29:32,986 --> 00:29:39,010
the reply at max tokens just like the
real API, and returns either a normal JSON

463
00:29:39,010 --> 00:29:42,172
message or a stream of server-sent events.

464
00:29:42,392 --> 00:29:48,077
Because the responses have the same
shape as the real API's, the SDK can't tell the

465
00:29:48,077 --> 00:29:48,830
difference.

466
00:29:49,050 --> 00:29:52,689
That means our tests
exercise the real SDK code.

467
00:29:53,989 --> 00:29:56,525
With a key, we create a normal client.

468
00:29:56,745 --> 00:30:01,785
The SDK reads the key from the
environment, so it never appears in our code.

469
00:30:02,005 --> 00:30:05,340
We pass the retry count
and a sixty-second timeout.

470
00:30:05,690 --> 00:30:12,034
Without a key, we print a clear offline
notice, and give the same SDK an HTTP client

471
00:30:12,034 --> 00:30:16,188
whose transport is our
fake API instead of the network.

472
00:30:17,488 --> 00:30:21,340
Settings come from
environment variables, as in Module 1.

473
00:30:21,560 --> 00:30:25,320
If there's no key, we switch
to offline mode automatically.

474
00:30:25,540 --> 00:30:31,011
The model defaults to Claude Opus
5.5, and the budget to fifty cents.

475
00:30:32,311 --> 00:30:37,820
The price table holds dollars per
million input and output tokens, for each model.

476
00:30:38,170 --> 00:30:41,475
Cost u s d is the
formula from lesson five.

477
00:30:41,695 --> 00:30:46,444
An unknown model raises an error
rather than silently costing zero.

478
00:30:46,794 --> 00:30:48,692
The cost tracker adds up spending.

479
00:30:48,912 --> 00:30:54,520
Its check method runs before every call,
and raises budget exceeded once the budget

480
00:30:54,520 --> 00:30:55,264
is used up.

481
00:30:55,484 --> 00:30:58,171
That's your protection
against a runaway loop.

482
00:30:58,521 --> 00:31:01,197
And add records the
cost of each finished call.

483
00:31:02,497 --> 00:31:07,863
An Answer bundles everything we learn
from a call: the text, the stop reason, the

484
00:31:07,863 --> 00:31:09,254
tokens, and the cost.

485
00:31:09,474 --> 00:31:12,884
Truncated is true when
the reply hit max tokens.

486
00:31:13,234 --> 00:31:19,102
Friendly errors is a context manager
that catches SDK exceptions, most specific

487
00:31:19,102 --> 00:31:22,444
first, and turns each
into one clear message.

488
00:31:22,664 --> 00:31:27,240
It's lesson five's error table,
written once and reused by every call.

489
00:31:27,590 --> 00:31:28,740
Next, fallbacks.

490
00:31:28,960 --> 00:31:33,861
If the model declines a request,
the API can re-run it on another model

491
00:31:33,861 --> 00:31:34,827
automatically.

492
00:31:35,047 --> 00:31:36,937
We opt in with this beta flag.

493
00:31:37,287 --> 00:31:43,035
And base builds the arguments every
request shares: the model, the beta flag, and

494
00:31:43,035 --> 00:31:48,570
fallbacks set to default, so the API
chooses a suitable fallback model for us.

495
00:31:48,920 --> 00:31:51,163
Answer turns a response into an Answer.

496
00:31:51,383 --> 00:31:57,034
It records the cost first, because even
a declined request is billed, then checks

497
00:31:57,034 --> 00:32:00,593
for a refusal, and only
then joins the text blocks.

498
00:32:01,893 --> 00:32:07,318
Explain checks the budget first, then
fills in the request template from prompts dot

499
00:32:07,318 --> 00:32:07,512
py.

500
00:32:07,862 --> 00:32:13,475
It opens a stream, inside friendly
errors, using the shared arguments, the tutor

501
00:32:13,475 --> 00:32:17,965
system prompt, and low effort,
which is plenty for explanations.

502
00:32:18,315 --> 00:32:23,946
Each piece of text is passed to an on
text callback as it arrives, so the assistant

503
00:32:23,946 --> 00:32:29,441
doesn't know or care whether it's
printed, shown in a web page, or collected by a

504
00:32:29,441 --> 00:32:29,781
test.

505
00:32:30,001 --> 00:32:32,516
Then the final message becomes an Answer.

506
00:32:33,816 --> 00:32:34,732
Let's run it.

507
00:32:34,952 --> 00:32:40,253
The offline notice comes first, then
the streamed placeholder, then a footer with

508
00:32:40,253 --> 00:32:45,685
the model, tokens, cost, and stop
reason, and finally the session total against the

509
00:32:45,685 --> 00:32:46,143
budget.

510
00:32:46,493 --> 00:32:51,818
With a tiny max tokens, the reply is
cut off, and the tool warns you instead of

511
00:32:51,818 --> 00:32:53,840
pretending everything is fine.

512
00:32:54,190 --> 00:32:59,604
And with a budget of zero, it refuses
before sending anything, with a one-line

513
00:32:59,604 --> 00:33:00,437
explanation.

514
00:33:01,737 --> 00:33:06,575
A fake API that speaks the real
protocol makes tests free and repeatable.

515
00:33:06,925 --> 00:33:11,025
One function decides between
the real and the offline client.

516
00:33:11,375 --> 00:33:15,484
Every call is costed, and a
budget check runs before each one.

517
00:33:15,834 --> 00:33:20,181
Errors are mapped once, in one
place, and fallbacks handle refusals.

518
00:33:20,531 --> 00:33:25,831
In part two: quizzes as structured
output, chat with history, the full demo, the

519
00:33:25,831 --> 00:33:27,619
tests, and the module quiz.

520
00:33:28,936 --> 00:33:29,961
Welcome back.

521
00:33:30,181 --> 00:33:31,540
Explanations work.

522
00:33:31,760 --> 00:33:37,109
Now we add quizzes as structured output,
a chat mode with trimmed history, and the

523
00:33:37,109 --> 00:33:39,392
command line that ties it together.

524
00:33:39,612 --> 00:33:43,806
Then we'll run the full demo, look
at the tests, and finish the module.

525
00:33:45,106 --> 00:33:50,804
A question has its text, exactly four
options, the index of the correct option, and

526
00:33:50,804 --> 00:33:53,070
an explanation of why it's right.

527
00:33:53,420 --> 00:33:56,256
A quiz is a topic
plus a list of questions.

528
00:33:56,476 --> 00:34:00,612
This one class becomes the
JSON schema the model must follow.

529
00:34:01,912 --> 00:34:07,164
Make quiz fills in the request: write N
multiple-choice questions about a topic.

530
00:34:07,514 --> 00:34:12,783
It calls parse with output format set
to Quiz, so the reply is constrained to the

531
00:34:12,783 --> 00:34:13,239
schema.

532
00:34:13,459 --> 00:34:19,158
Quizzes need more care than
explanations, so we use medium effort, and a generous

533
00:34:19,158 --> 00:34:19,932
max tokens.

534
00:34:20,282 --> 00:34:26,065
If the reply somehow doesn't fit the
schema, for example three options instead of

535
00:34:26,065 --> 00:34:31,919
four, the SDK's validation raises a
pydantic validation error, and we turn it into

536
00:34:31,919 --> 00:34:33,204
one clear message.

537
00:34:33,554 --> 00:34:39,431
Finally, if the quiz was cut off by max
tokens, we refuse to return a half-finished

538
00:34:39,431 --> 00:34:42,618
quiz, and suggest
asking for fewer questions.

539
00:34:43,918 --> 00:34:48,726
For trimming, a quick local estimate
is good enough: about four characters per

540
00:34:48,726 --> 00:34:49,095
token.

541
00:34:49,315 --> 00:34:53,660
When you need an exact number, use
the token counting endpoint instead.

542
00:34:54,010 --> 00:34:57,958
A chat session holds the
history list and a token budget for it.

543
00:34:58,308 --> 00:35:03,570
Trim drops the oldest user and
assistant pair while the history is over budget,

544
00:35:03,570 --> 00:35:05,901
exactly the rule from lesson three.

545
00:35:06,121 --> 00:35:08,817
It always keeps at
least the latest exchange.

546
00:35:09,167 --> 00:35:14,071
Send appends the question, trims, and
calls the model with the whole history.

547
00:35:14,291 --> 00:35:19,888
If the call fails, it removes the
unanswered question, so the history never ends up

548
00:35:19,888 --> 00:35:21,844
with two user turns in a row.

549
00:35:22,064 --> 00:35:23,524
Then the reply is stored.

550
00:35:24,824 --> 00:35:28,477
For explain, the on text
callback prints each piece immediately.

551
00:35:28,697 --> 00:35:33,812
The footer with tokens and cost goes
to standard error, so it never mixes into

552
00:35:33,812 --> 00:35:35,386
output you save or pipe.

553
00:35:35,736 --> 00:35:40,911
Quiz prints the questions with letters,
optionally saves the JSON, and grades your

554
00:35:40,911 --> 00:35:42,489
answers if you pass them.

555
00:35:42,839 --> 00:35:48,409
Chat reads one message per line from
standard input, so it works interactively, and

556
00:35:48,409 --> 00:35:50,556
from a file for demos and tests.

557
00:35:50,906 --> 00:35:56,386
And main builds the settings, the
client, and the assistant, and turns any study

558
00:35:56,386 --> 00:36:01,455
error into one clean line and exit
code one, just like Module 1's toolkit.

559
00:36:02,755 --> 00:36:04,570
A two-question quiz, graded.

560
00:36:04,790 --> 00:36:10,276
These are the fake API's placeholder
questions, but they went through exactly the

561
00:36:10,276 --> 00:36:14,407
same structured output path:
schema, parsing, and validation.

562
00:36:14,757 --> 00:36:16,976
And a chat with two messages piped in.

563
00:36:17,196 --> 00:36:21,415
Look at the second reply: the
request carried two earlier messages.

564
00:36:21,635 --> 00:36:23,162
That's our history at work.

565
00:36:23,512 --> 00:36:26,756
The tokens command
uses the counting endpoint.

566
00:36:26,976 --> 00:36:29,084
Offline, it's labelled as an estimate.

567
00:36:30,384 --> 00:36:31,792
Now the failure paths.

568
00:36:32,012 --> 00:36:37,608
A single rate limit: the SDK retries
automatically and the explanation still

569
00:36:37,608 --> 00:36:38,197
arrives.

570
00:36:38,547 --> 00:36:43,838
Three rate limits in a row exhausts
the two retries, and we get one clear error

571
00:36:43,838 --> 00:36:45,312
line, not a traceback.

572
00:36:45,662 --> 00:36:48,317
And a refusal is reported plainly too.

573
00:36:49,617 --> 00:36:52,501
The tests use the
fake API as a test double.

574
00:36:52,721 --> 00:36:57,856
This one checks that the explanation
arrived in pieces, that the pieces add up to

575
00:36:57,856 --> 00:37:03,055
the final text, and, by inspecting what
the fake received, that the request really

576
00:37:03,055 --> 00:37:06,605
was streamed, with
fallbacks, and with the right prompt.

577
00:37:06,955 --> 00:37:10,313
This test proves max
tokens truncation is detected.

578
00:37:10,663 --> 00:37:16,317
And these two test the SDK's real retry
behaviour: two failures then success makes

579
00:37:16,317 --> 00:37:21,213
three requests, and three rate
limits in a row becomes our clean error.

580
00:37:22,513 --> 00:37:26,172
Twenty-three tests, all
passing, with no key and no network.

581
00:37:26,392 --> 00:37:31,815
A conftest fixture also removes any real
key from the environment during tests, so

582
00:37:31,815 --> 00:37:33,799
they can never cost you money.

583
00:37:34,149 --> 00:37:39,254
And mypy is happy with every type
hint, including the SDK's own types.

584
00:37:40,554 --> 00:37:46,153
If your key is rejected, check the
environment variable: no stray quotes or spaces,

585
00:37:46,153 --> 00:37:48,986
not revoked, and from the right workspace.

586
00:37:49,336 --> 00:37:54,464
If you expected real answers but see
offline mode, Python can't see the key.

587
00:37:54,684 --> 00:37:56,765
Set it in the same terminal session.

588
00:37:57,115 --> 00:38:01,436
Repeated rate limits mean you're
sending too fast for your account's limits.

589
00:38:01,656 --> 00:38:03,883
Slow down, or allow more retries.

590
00:38:04,233 --> 00:38:08,162
And for cut-off answers,
raise max tokens, or ask for less.

591
00:38:09,462 --> 00:38:14,441
Language models predict tokens, and
tokens drive cost, limits, and speed.

592
00:38:14,791 --> 00:38:19,555
You called the API and read
content blocks, stop reasons, and usage.

593
00:38:19,905 --> 00:38:23,464
You managed stateless
conversations, with trimmed history.

594
00:38:23,814 --> 00:38:27,561
You streamed replies, and
got validated structured output.

595
00:38:27,911 --> 00:38:33,487
And you handled errors, retries,
refusals, costs, and budgets, in a fully tested

596
00:38:33,487 --> 00:38:34,044
project.

597
00:38:35,344 --> 00:38:37,173
Module quiz, question one.

598
00:38:37,393 --> 00:38:40,292
Why do output tokens
matter so much for cost?

599
00:38:45,992 --> 00:38:49,507
They usually cost several
times more than input tokens.

600
00:38:49,727 --> 00:38:52,848
Five times more, on Claude Opus 5.5.

601
00:38:54,148 --> 00:38:55,184
Question two.

602
00:38:55,404 --> 00:38:58,146
What does a stop
reason of max tokens tell you?

603
00:39:03,846 --> 00:39:07,470
The reply was cut off at
your limit, and may be incomplete.

604
00:39:08,770 --> 00:39:09,832
Question three.

605
00:39:10,052 --> 00:39:12,664
A chat bot forgets
everything said earlier.

606
00:39:12,884 --> 00:39:14,424
What's the most likely bug?

607
00:39:20,124 --> 00:39:23,748
The app isn't sending the
earlier messages with each request.

608
00:39:23,968 --> 00:39:25,519
The API is stateless.

609
00:39:26,819 --> 00:39:27,882
Question four.

610
00:39:28,102 --> 00:39:30,680
Which errors are worth
retrying automatically?

611
00:39:36,380 --> 00:39:39,413
Rate limits, server
errors, and connection errors.

612
00:39:39,633 --> 00:39:42,489
And the SDK already retries them for you.

613
00:39:43,789 --> 00:39:45,051
And question five.

614
00:39:45,271 --> 00:39:48,737
What does parse with output
format equals Quiz give you?

615
00:39:54,437 --> 00:39:56,284
A validated Quiz object.

616
00:39:56,504 --> 00:39:59,577
The structure is
guaranteed, but the facts are not.

617
00:39:59,797 --> 00:40:02,510
A valid quiz can still
contain a wrong answer.

618
00:40:03,810 --> 00:40:05,316
Your extension challenge.

619
00:40:05,536 --> 00:40:09,769
Pick one or more, and write a
test for each, using the fake API.

620
00:40:10,119 --> 00:40:14,761
Add a model option, and compare
costs between Opus, Sonnet, and Haiku.

621
00:40:15,111 --> 00:40:18,141
Show the exact token
count before each chat turn.

622
00:40:18,491 --> 00:40:22,787
Add an interactive quiz mode that
shows the explanations at the end.

623
00:40:23,137 --> 00:40:29,021
Or save chat history to a file and
reload it next time, a first step towards Module

624
00:40:29,021 --> 00:40:29,163
6.

625
00:40:30,463 --> 00:40:35,336
These are the official
documentation pages for everything in this module.

626
00:40:35,556 --> 00:40:38,945
Links are in the description,
along with the full source code.

627
00:40:43,045 --> 00:40:44,784
That's Module 2 complete.

628
00:40:45,004 --> 00:40:50,700
You can now call a language model from
Python reliably, affordably, and testably.

629
00:40:50,920 --> 00:40:56,698
In Module 3, you'll learn to write
prompts that work consistently, validate every

630
00:40:56,698 --> 00:41:02,619
output with Pydantic, version your
prompts, and evaluate them, while building an AI

631
00:41:02,619 --> 00:41:03,760
Resume Analyzer.

632
00:41:03,980 --> 00:41:04,843
See you there.
